	/* ============ LAYOUT ============
	   Rail left, card right, both stretched to the same row height — the rail sets it and
	   the card follows, so the two panels always end on the same line. */
	/* The row carries the height, not the rail. Put it on the rail instead and the card is
	   free to be sized by its own content — which is the portrait — so a wide column made
	   a tall plate, a tall plate made a tall card, and the card ran hundreds of pixels
	   past the rail beside it. Sized here, both children stretch to the same definite
	   number and the plate is bounded instead of doing the bounding.

	   The height tracks vw as well as vh, which looks odd until you follow what sets the
	   plate: its width comes from the column, the column from the card, the card from the
	   viewport's WIDTH — so on a narrow desktop a tall row is guaranteed dead panel around
	   a small photograph. Capping against 44vw keeps the leftover roughly even across
	   sizes instead of piling it all up at 1280. */
	.tm-shell {
		--row: clamp(470px, min(64vh, 44vw), 700px);
